[107.169988, "o", "    ✓ todo — 3/5 done · STEP 4: Clean up\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /tmp/piptest 2>&1 && echo \"cleaned up /tmp/piptest\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /tmp/piptest 2>&1 && echo \"cleaned up /tmp/piptest\"\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 24 tool call(s) so far\r\n  [tools] 24 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 24 tool call(s) so far\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 25 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ todo \r\n    ✓ todo — 5/5 done\r\n  ⚙ task \r\n    ✓ task implementor — 25 tool call(s)\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  [tools] 12 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\nDone — pip is working again, verified against PyPI.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong.** Not PEP 668, not permissions. The active interpreter is `/usr/local/bin/python3.13`, and its `site-packages` had `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` and the `/usr/local/bin/pip` launcher scripts, but no `pip/` package directory. So every invocation died before doing"]
[107.170026, "o", " anything:\r\n\r\n```\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker on this Debian image looks like the culprit and isn't — the running interpreter is 3.13 under `/usr/local` and PEP 668 never reads that path.\r\n\r\n**The fix.** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip` did *not* repair it, and that's the trap: the orphaned `dist-info` makes ensurepip believe pip is already satisfied, so it reports success and changes nothing. The working repair was to unpack the bundled `pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` from `ensurepip/_bundled/` directly into `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`.\r\n\r\n**Verification.** `import pip` gives 24.3.1, and a real install against the index completed:\r\n\r\n```\r\nCollecting six\r\n  Downloading six-1.17.0-py2.py3-none-any.whl (11 kB)\r\nSuccessfully installed six-1.17.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nTwo caveats worth knowing. The damage lives in the image layer, so a container rebuild will bring the orphaned `dist-info` back — the durable fix is at buil"]
[107.170036, "o", "d time, not in the running container. And I made no repository changes: the fault was in the interpreter, not in this checkout.\r\n\r\nA note on *how* this got done, since it affects how much to trust the log: the `coordinator` role I run as has no `bash` (`.gnomon/roles.toml:32`), so the two shell-level steps ran as `implementor` sub-turns. The diagnostic evidence and the repair output above are theirs, not mine — I checked the reasoning against the surface's own role definitions but did not watch the commands execute.\r\n\r\n[result] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=12 surface=f37e7204be91\r\n"]
